WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.458
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: One of the
biggest challenges in machine

00:00:02.458 --> 00:00:04.890
learning is building a
system to continuously

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:08.310
evaluate and update your
models to reflect those changes

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:09.840
in the environment.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.170
Doing this can help ensure
your project's success.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.470
But how do you do that?

00:00:14.470 --> 00:00:17.190
Well, that is what this
video is all about.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.630
I am Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this video

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.610
I'll walk you through a demo of
the end-to-end MLOps workflow

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:25.560
using Vertex AI pipelines.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:27.750
In the last episode,
I introduced

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:31.560
MLOps and the different
steps in the ML workflow.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.325
Let's revisit that.

00:00:33.325 --> 00:00:35.700
[REWIND NOISE]

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.850
First step is data
transformation,

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:41.700
feature engineering,
and model development.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.380
Then comes continuous training
of the model and finding ways

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.420
to automate training
and making it

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.820
repeatable, so
your ML system can

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.960
respond to newly available data
and other triggers in your ML

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:55.800
models.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.280
Then deploying those
models to make predictions.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.670
And while the model is in
production, continuously

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:06.420
monitoring it so that
you can make sure

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.610
that performance is improving.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:12.810
And finally, we have model
management or governance

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:16.450
capabilities that span
across the entire framework.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.190
And we need traceability,
verifiability, and auditability

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.710
of every single step
of this workflow, which

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:24.330
happens in model management.

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:30.510
Now, Vertex Pipelines helps us
automate this MLOps workflow.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:33.870
And it does that by
orchestrating the workflow

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.580
in a serverless manner.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.980
It is based on containers.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:42.300
And as we know, containers
are portable and scalable,

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.610
and it supports
both KFP and TFX.

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.430
Each step takes an
input and provides

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:54.970
an output which acts as an
input for the subsequent step.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:56.790
But before we jump
into the demo,

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:59.160
let's see the steps
that we will take.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:04.560
Now, our pipeline will create
a data set in Vertex AI.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.920
Then we would use
this tabular data set

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:13.230
and apply a classification
model on it using AutoML,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.020
and then get evaluation
metrics on that model.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:19.830
And based on those
metrics, we would decide

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.630
if we want to deploy the model.

00:02:22.630 --> 00:02:25.830
So there'll be a conditional
logic in Vertex pipelines

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.420
that will help us decide this.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.280
And then finally,
deploy the model

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:32.850
to an endpoint using
Vertex predictions.

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.780
Now let's see how this works.

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.380
The first step is to
create a new notebook

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:42.420
instance with TensorFlow
Enterprise 2.3 without GPUs.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.860
I've already created one here,
so I'll just get into this one.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.220
I installed the AI platform
and the Pipeline components

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.300
package.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.300
Then we need to
restart the kernel

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:58.500
and check if the packages
are correctly installed.

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:02.340
Then I set the project ID
and cloud storage bucket

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.060
that we will be using throughout
this pipeline, followed

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.440
by the relevant library
imports, and defining

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:13.950
some constants, such as
the region and pipeline

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.400
route, which is the path
where the artifacts created

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.010
by our pipeline will be written.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.260
After this initial
setup, I first

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.740
define the custom evaluation
component in our pipeline.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:29.850
This component will get
the evaluation metrics

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:33.360
from the trained AutoML
classification model,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:37.920
parse the metrics, and render
them in Vertex Pipeline's UI,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.350
compare the metrics
to a threshold

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:43.530
to determine whether the
model should be deployed.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.440
And then before we
define the components,

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.400
let's understand its input
and output parameters.

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.820
As input, this pipeline
takes some metadata

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.710
on our cloud project--

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:59.850
the resulting trained model,
which we will define later,

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:04.060
the model's evaluation
metrics, and a threshold,

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.920
which we will define
when we run our pipeline.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.380
In the case of this
classification model,

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:15.120
threshold string will be the
area under the ROC curve value

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.620
for which we should
deploy the model.

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.300
For example, if we
pass in 0.95, that

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.820
means we would only
want our pipeline

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:28.940
to deploy the model if
this metric is above 95%.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.030
Our evaluation component
returns true or false,

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:36.720
indicating whether or
not to deploy our model.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.980
Now step two in the process is
to add our pre-built components

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.660
of the pipeline.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.030
First, I defined
the display name

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.850
of the pipeline, then
defined the input

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:50.040
parameters of our pipeline.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:53.670
The rest of the pipeline uses
a few pre-built components

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:57.120
for interacting with
Vertex AI services.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.490
TabularDataCreateOp creates a
tabular data set in Vertex AI,

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:04.650
and gives the data
set source either

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.140
in cloud storage or BigQuery.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:10.950
In this pipeline we're passing
the data via BigQuery table

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.750
URL.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.510
AutoMLTableTrainingJobRunOp.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:19.920
This method kicks off an AutoML
training job for a tabular data

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:20.610
set.

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:24.270
We pass a few configuration
parameters to this component,

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.470
including the model type,
which is classification here,

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:31.170
some data on the
columns and how long we

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:35.370
would like to run training for,
and pointers to the data set.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:37.890
To pass in the data
set to this component,

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:41.160
we are providing the output
of the previous component.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:46.020
ModelDeployOp deploys the model
to an endpoint in Vertex AI.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.120
There are additional
configuration options

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:51.270
available, but here, we are
providing the endpoint machine

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:54.900
type, project, and models
we would like to deploy.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.970
And the model is the
output of the training

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.650
step in our pipeline.

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.020
Like I mentioned earlier,
in this pipeline,

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:04.860
we're making use of conditional
logic, a feature of Vertex

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.650
Pipelines that lets
you define a condition,

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:10.110
along with different
branches based

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.420
on the result of that condition.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:16.110
In our pipeline definition, we
passed our threshold parameter.

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:18.420
This is the accuracy
threshold we're

00:06:18.420 --> 00:06:22.020
using to determine whether to
deploy our model to an endpoint

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:23.070
or not.

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:26.580
To implement this, we make
use of the condition class

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.650
from Kubeflow SDK.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:32.230
Remember that custom model
evaluation component I created?

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.290
That's the one
we're passing here.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:36.750
If this condition is
true, the pipeline

00:06:36.750 --> 00:06:39.940
will continue to execute
the deploy component.

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:42.270
Otherwise, the
pipeline will stop here

00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:44.850
and won't deploy the model.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:48.000
Now, the next step
is to compile and run

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.710
the end-to-end pipeline.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:53.370
For this, we use the compile
function and pass the package

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:55.660
path and the pipeline to it.

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.900
And then finally,
we run the pipeline.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.610
It will take a little
over an hour to run.

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:02.800
But I have one
that I ran earlier,

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.130
so let me show you that one.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.210
Here's our pipeline.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:10.240
If I toggle the Expand
Artifact button at the top,

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:13.110
we see details of the
different artifacts

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:15.000
created from our pipeline.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.010
If I click on the
dataset artifact,

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:21.000
it shows the Vertex AI data
sets that were created.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:24.000
I'm curious about the
custom metric component

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.240
that we created.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:27.030
So if you click
on this artifact,

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:30.960
we see the confusion
matrix for this model.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.720
Vertex Pipelines
also lets us track

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:37.020
lineage of the artifacts created
throughout our pipelines.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:40.920
This can help us understand
where artifacts were created

00:07:40.920 --> 00:07:45.180
and how they are being used
throughout our ML workflow.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.700
If we run this pipeline
multiple times,

00:07:47.700 --> 00:07:51.120
we may want to compare
metrics across runs.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:54.390
I'm doing that here
to access run metadata

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.910
for all runs of this
pipeline and load it

00:07:56.910 --> 00:07:59.160
into a pandas dataframe.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:02.670
You've now learned how to
build, run, and get metadata

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:04.980
for an end-to-end
machine learning pipeline

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:06.960
on Vertex Pipelines.

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:08.040
What's next?

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:09.510
Well, try it out yourself.

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.170
The codelab is linked below.

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:13.230
And if you run into
questions, reach out

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:17.450
to me in the comments or
@PVergadia on Twitter.

